{
  "id": "COMPOUND:30040 - PROTEIN:5836",
  "verbalization": "Basiliskamide B is implicated in the interaction with CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, a protein that is targeted by multiple complex molecules, including (1R,8S,9R,16R)-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-9-[(1R,8S,9R,16R)-4,6,12-trihydroxy-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaen-9-yl]-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaene-4,6,12-triol and (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan. These molecules are all linked to HUVEC, a human vascular endothelial cell line, suggesting a potential role for Basiliskamide B in modulating HUVEC function through this protein. The evidence indicates that Basiliskamide B may act as a modulator of HUVEC activity via a complex pathway involving sialyltransferase, which is a key enzyme in the synthesis of sialic acid. The integration of these pathways highlights the involvement of sialyltransferase in the biological effects of Basiliskamide B, potentially through the regulation of cell surface glycosylation and subsequent signaling events.",
